---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import Steps from "../../components/use-cases/Steps.astro";
import Benefits from "../../components/use-cases/Benefits.astro";
import Note from "../../components/use-cases/Note.astro";
import { Shield, FileText, Users, Search } from "lucide-react";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Private AI Meeting Notes for Healthcare Teams on Mac | Summit"
  description="Notes for care team, case review and admin meetings on your Mac. Local transcription, no meeting bot, and cloud AI only if your organization approves it."
  audience="Healthcare Teams"
  current="healthcare"
  heading="Private meeting notes for healthcare teams"
  lead="Care team huddles, case reviews, research meetings and practice operations all produce decisions someone has to find later. Summit turns recorded meetings into transcripts and summaries on your Mac, without a bot joining the call."
  ctaHeading="Try Summit on your next team meeting"
  ctaText="Summit runs on macOS. Choose Local Models in AI settings to keep summaries and chat on your Mac."
>
  <Note title="A meeting notes app, not a medical scribe">
    Summit is built for meetings. It is not a clinical documentation tool, does
    not connect to an EHR and is not certified for any healthcare regulation.
    Document patient encounters with the tools your organization has approved,
    and check with your compliance team before recording anything that includes
    patient information.
  </Note>

  <h2>You choose where meeting audio and text are processed</h2>
  <p>
    Transcription runs on your Mac, and there is no Summit account holding your
    meeting library. Select Local Models and summaries and chat run on your Mac
    too. If you choose a cloud AI provider, it receives transcript excerpts and,
    for chat, your question with related topics, action items and meeting titles
    and dates, so confirm with your organization whether that provider is
    approved for the meetings you record. Markdown auto-export and iCloud sync
    are off until you turn them on.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app does not separately encrypt its local database or recordings; they
    rely on macOS protections, so turn on FileVault and keep your Mac account
    locked down.
  </p>
  <p>
    Patient consent, organizational policy and privacy law still apply. The
    <a href="/security/architecture">architecture page</a> lists exactly what is
    stored and what can leave your Mac.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep each team's meetings organized</h2>
  <Steps
    steps={[
      {
        title: "Create a folder for each team or project",
        body: 'Give each committee, unit or study its own folder, and optionally a folder <a href="/docs/guides/summary-templates">summary template</a> so its meetings are written up the same way.',
      },
      {
        title: "Use clear meeting titles",
        body: "Name meetings with the team and topic, so the right conversation is easy to spot in search results.",
      },
      {
        title: "Check the scope before you ask",
        body: "Filter search by folder, or select the meetings you need and chat about just those. The chat shows which meetings are included before you ask a question.",
      },
      {
        title: "Open the original passage",
        body: "Chat answers cite timestamped transcript passages. Check the source before an answer goes into minutes, a protocol or a report.",
      },
    ]}
  />
  <Note>
    Folders help organize notes; they are not a separate account, encrypted
    vault or access-control boundary.
  </Note>

  <h2>Why healthcare teams choose Summit</h2>
  <Benefits
    benefits={[
      {
        icon: Shield,
        title: "No meeting bot",
        body: "Summit records from your Mac instead of joining the call as a participant, for video calls and in-person meetings alike.",
      },
      {
        icon: FileText,
        title: "Start from a draft",
        body: "Summit drafts a summary with key points and action items after each recording, so minutes start from notes instead of a blank page. Review them before they are shared.",
      },
      {
        icon: Users,
        title: "Consistent team write-ups",
        body: "Summary templates shape notes for case reviews, committee minutes and project updates.",
      },
      {
        icon: Search,
        title: "Recall with sources",
        body: "Search past meetings for specific decisions or follow-ups, and follow chat citations back to the passage in the transcript.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Meetings Summit fits</h2>
  <h3>Care team and case review meetings</h3>
  <p>
    Keep track of decisions and owners from multidisciplinary meetings, where
    your organization's policy allows recording them.
  </p>
  <h3>Practice and hospital operations</h3>
  <p>
    Document staffing, quality improvement, vendor and planning meetings, and
    find what was agreed before the next one.
  </p>
  <h3>Research and study meetings</h3>
  <p>
    Keep a folder per study for protocol discussions, lab meetings and
    investigator calls.
  </p>
  <h3>Health coaches and consultants</h3>
  <p>
    Keep notes from client and partner meetings organized by engagement, with
    recording consent from everyone present.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you record in a healthcare setting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Confirm with your compliance team which meetings may be recorded and
      whether patient information may be discussed in them
    </li>
    <li>Tell participants you are recording and get consent</li>
    <li>
      Select Local Models, or a cloud AI provider your organization has approved
    </li>
    <li>Turn on FileVault and use a locked macOS account</li>
    <li>
      Review AI-generated notes against the transcript before sharing them
    </li>
  </ul>
</UseCaseLayout>
